 you.","verse":9},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"For who has despised the day of small things? But these seven eyes of the LORD, which scan the whole earth, will rejoice when they see the plumb line in the hand of Zerubbabel.”","verse":10}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["God’s Commitment to His People"],"sourceClass":"theme-title","t":"h"},{"c":["Sometimes, from the perspective of God’s people, it seems that God is being slow to fulfill his promises. Abraham and Sarah had to wait twenty-five years from the time God first promised to make of them a great nation until their son Isaac was born. After God promised the kingship to David, David had to endure many trials under the reign of Saul before God finally established him as king. And though God had promised to bring a remnant back to the Promised Land after the Exile, the Jewish captives had to wait seventy years to return. Yet in each case, God indeed brought about the fulfillment of his promises."],"sourceClass":"theme-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Zechariah prophesied around the time that the Temple was being rebuilt after the Exile. At one point during the reconstruction, God told Zechariah not to “despise these small beginnings” (",{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.10","r":"Zech.4.10","rawR":"Zech.4.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.10","value":"Zech.4.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Zech 4:10"},"). God had acted in the past for the ultimate good of his people, even in the exile to Babylon (see ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.7.12-14","r":"Zech.7.12-14","rawR":"Zech.7.12-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.7.12-14","value":"Zech.7.12-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:12-14"},"). God would accomplish his purposes for Israel’s well-being in his own time and his own way. The people of Israel could take courage in the present and have hope for the future because God keeps his word and fulfills his promises (",{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.9","r":"Zech.4.9","rawR":"Zech.4.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.9","value":"Zech.4.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:9"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["God wants patience and faith from his people, not a strident voice. God kept his word and fulfilled the promises he made indicating that his people would return to Jerusalem and that the Temple would be rebuilt. About five hundred years after Zechariah, God fulfilled the promises that he would bring salvation for all people, by sending Jesus the Messiah into the world. Likewise, Jesus’ words that he will one day return and fully establish his Kingdom can be trusted (see ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.24.27","r":"Matt.24.27","rawR":"Matt.24.27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.24.27","value":"Matt.24.27"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 24:27"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.24.30","r":"Matt.24.30","rawR":"Matt.24.30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.24.30","value":"Matt.24.30"}],"t":"ref","v":"30"},"; see also ",{"href":"?bref=Acts.1.6-11","r":"Acts.1.6-11","rawR":"Acts.1.6-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Acts.1.6-11","value":"Acts.1.6-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Acts 1:6-11"},")."],"sourceClass":"theme-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"theme-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Sam.15.29","r":"1Sam.15.29","rawR":"1Sam.15.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.15.29","value":"1Sam.15.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sam 15:29"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.7.28-29","r":"2Sam.7.28-29","rawR":"2Sam.7.28-29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.7.28-29","value":"2Sam.7.28-29"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sam 7:28-29"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.36.21","r":"2Chr.36.21","rawR":"2Chr.36.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.36.21","value":"2Chr.36.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 36:21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.42.1-9","r":"Isa.42.1-9","rawR":"Isa.42.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.42.1-9","value":"Isa.42.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 42:1-9"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.49.15","r":"Isa.49.15","rawR":"Isa.49.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.49.15","value":"Isa.49.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"49:15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.2.13","r":"Zech.2.13","rawR":"Zech.2.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.2.13","value":"Zech.2.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Zech 2:13"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.9-10","r":"Zech.4.9-10","rawR":"Zech.4.9-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.9-10","value":"Zech.4.9-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:9-10"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.14.3","r":"Zech.14.3","rawR":"Zech.14.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.14.3","value":"Zech.14.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"14:3"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.14.1-4","r":"John.14.1-4","rawR":"John.14.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.14.1-4","value":"John.14.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"John 14:1-4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.17.17","r":"John.17.17","rawR":"John.17.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.17.17","value":"John.17.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"17:17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Acts.1.6-11","r":"Acts.1.6-11","rawR":"Acts.1.6-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Acts.1.6-11","value":"Acts.1.6-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Acts 1:6-11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Titus.1.2","r":"Titus.1.2","rawR":"Titus.1.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Titus.1.2","value":"Titus.1.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Titus 1:2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Rev.21.5","r":"Rev.21.5","rawR":"Rev.21.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Rev.21.5","value":"Rev.21.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Rev 21:5"}],"sourceClass":"theme-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"ThemeNote:GodsCommitmentToHisPeople","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"GodsCommitmentToHisPeople","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"ThemeNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"06eaaad44d3dd9e287d78394e7819fe052f84eb6173c8f5279c7ddec9c3d369d","sourceItemName":"GodsCommitmentToHisPeople","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/ThemeNotes.xml","sourceOrder":184,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.4.9-10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"God’s Commitment to His People","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"God’s Commitment to His People","type":"ThemeNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.1.7-6.15","r":"Zech.1.7-6.15","rawR":"Zech.1.7-6.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.1.7-6.15","value":"Zech.1.7-6.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:7–6:15"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," These eight visions addressed the Judeans’ fears that prevented them from responding in faith to God’s promises. These visions, which occurred two months after Haggai’s final two messages (",{"href":"?bref=Hagg.2.10-19","r":"Hagg.2.10-19","rawR":"Hagg.2.10-19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hagg.2.10-19","value":"Hagg.2.10-19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Hag 2:10-19"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Hagg.2.20-23","r":"Hagg.2.20-23","rawR":"Hagg.2.20-23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hagg.2.20-23","value":"Hagg.2.20-23"}],"t":"ref","v":"20-23"},"), suggest that Haggai’s prophecies were being fulfilled."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.1.7-6.15","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":6,"endVerse":15,"startChapter":1,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.1.7-6.15","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ebdf3bb0f9a43a2f6405ddb69741057dd776f10a855e0f493173d69a8995941c","sourceItemName":"Zech.1.7-6.15","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11505,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.1.7-6.15","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.1.7-6.15","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.1-14","r":"Zech.4.1-14","rawR":"Zech.4.1-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.1-14","value":"Zech.4.1-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1-14"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This vision affirms that through his Spirit, God would enable Zerubbabel and Jeshua to rebuild the Temple."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.4.1-14","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":14,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.4.1-14","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"dd079b56d133ba36d5d22fcb0ef90a855e7cd82111dc143a4f6a60e2f0922c6c","sourceItemName":"Zech.4.1-14","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11534,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.4.1-14","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.4.1-14","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.1","r":"Zech.4.1","rawR":"Zech.4.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.1","value":"Zech.4.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["woke me:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The series of visions (",{"href":"?bref=Zech.1.7-6.8","r":"Zech.1.7-6.8","rawR":"Zech.1.7-6.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.1.7-6.8","value":"Zech.1.7-6.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"1:7–6:8"},") came to Zechariah in a single night. The prophet slept briefly before this fifth vision."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.4.1","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.4.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e9aa022a5f8aba248c3cc7dcd2aebee9a4d4a36ee21c03347577bf49546854c5","sourceItemName":"Zech.4.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11535,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.4.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.4.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.2-3","r":"Zech.4.2-3","rawR":"Zech.4.2-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.2-3","value":"Zech.4.2-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:2-3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["gold lampstand:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The golden menorah of the Tabernacle (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.25.31","r":"Exod.25.31","rawR":"Exod.25.31","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.25.31","value":"Exod.25.31"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 25:31"},") was duplicated in the new Temple as a symbol of God’s purity and holiness and the light of his revelation. • The ",{"c":["two olive trees"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," represent the offices of priest and king in Israel; they are emblems for Jeshua and Zerubbabel. The golden ",{"c":["oil"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (see also ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.12-14","r":"Zech.4.12-14","rawR":"Zech.4.12-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.12-14","value":"Zech.4.12-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Zech 4:12-14"},") represents God’s Holy Spirit."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.4.2-3","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.4.2-3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"458733547ad63c972a6cb510acabaa3cc1f6d20f9bb63b86839df0c9fe395e1a","sourceItemName":"Zech.4.2-3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11536,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.4.2-3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.4.2-3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.6","r":"Zech.4.6","rawR":"Zech.4.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.6","value":"Zech.4.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Jeshua and Zerubbabel would have success in the rebuilding of the Jerusalem Temple, not by their own ",{"c":["force"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," or ",{"c":["strength"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," but because of the presence of God’s empowering ",{"c":["Spirit"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Hagg.2.4-5","r":"Hagg.2.4-5","rawR":"Hagg.2.4-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hagg.2.4-5","value":"Hagg.2.4-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Hag 2:4-5"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.4.6","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.4.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e777b21c70a10f863f77707d6dcfb789068357ade57a3263b2dd62b7e863aee3","sourceItemName":"Zech.4.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11537,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.4.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.4.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.7","r":"Zech.4.7","rawR":"Zech.4.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.7","value":"Zech.4.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:7"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["A mighty mountain . . . will become a level plain"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is a figure of speech in both Old Testament (see ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.40.4","r":"Isa.40.4","rawR":"Isa.40.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.40.4","value":"Isa.40.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Isa 40:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.49.11","r":"Isa.49.11","rawR":"Isa.49.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.49.11","value":"Isa.49.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"49:11"},") and New Testament (see ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.17.20","r":"Matt.17.20","rawR":"Matt.17.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.17.20","value":"Matt.17.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 17:20"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Cor.13.2","r":"1Cor.13.2","rawR":"1Cor.13.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Cor.13.2","value":"1Cor.13.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Cor 13:2"},"). Faith in God enables his servants to overcome seemingly impossible obstacles. • The capstone or headstone (",{"c":["final stone"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},") completes a stone wall or building (see ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.118.22","r":"Ps.118.22","rawR":"Ps.118.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.118.22","value":"Ps.118.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 118:22"},"). • ",{"c":["May God bless it!:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This prayer seeks God’s favor and blessing on the new Temple."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.4.7","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":7,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.4.7","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"90a9e883c8c0e23ab584066ac8501b0e3325c022a78ae2d669181abe894bca76","sourceItemName":"Zech.4.7","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11538,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.4.7","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.4.7","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.10","r":"Zech.4.10","rawR":"Zech.4.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.10","value":"Zech.4.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," A ",{"c":["plumb line,"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," a cord with a tin or lead weight attached to one end, is used in construction to ensure that a wall is vertical. • ",{"c":["The seven lamps:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Seven"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," signifies completeness or perfection. God sees all that takes place on earth; he controls the fates of peoples and nations."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.4.10","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":10}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.4.10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"9ea46880f2f5c73e06776587b0852ddb92050057843c67fe7483918dfd9ba5ca","sourceItemName":"Zech.4.10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11539,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.4.10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.4.10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.14","r":"Zech.4.14","rawR":"Zech.4.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.14","value":"Zech.4.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:14"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["two anointed ones:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Jeshua and Zerubbabel, the religious and civic leaders of Jerusalem after the return from exile (",{"href":"?bref=Zech.3.1","r":"Zech.3.1","rawR":"Zech.3.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.3.1","value":"Zech.3.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:1"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.3.7","r":"Zech.3.7","rawR":"Zech.3.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.3.7","value":"Zech.3.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Zech.4.6-7","r":"Zech.4.6-7","rawR":"Zech.4.6-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Zech.4.6-7","value":"Zech.4.6-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:6-7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Hagg.1.14","r":"Hagg.1.14","rawR":"Hagg.1.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hagg.1.14","value":"Hagg.1.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Hag 1:14"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Hagg.2.2-3","r":"Hagg.2.2-3","rawR":"Hagg.2.2-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Hagg.2.2-3","value":"Hagg.2.2-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:2-3"},"), were anointed with oil as part of their commissioning. Under their leadership, the Temple of the Lord was rebuilt and worship was restored in Jerusalem."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Zech.4.14","refs":[{"book":"ZEC","endChapter":4,"endVerse":14,"startChapter":4,"startVerse":14}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zech.4.14","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"ZEC","sourceChapterCoverage":[4],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"422f288528cdf74e9eb0a1f467b0d059ae3e9a418b0c093c1c19e4cbf5fb1cfc","sourceItemName":"Zech.4.14","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":11540,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"ZEC","canonicalOSIS":"Zech","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Zech"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Zech.4.14","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Zech.4.14","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}